A repo with Rust files now gets an advisory naming the better backend and,
in the same breath, the trust implication of turning it on. Emitted whether
or not the binary is installed -- someone deciding whether to install it
deserves the caveat BEFORE they do.

    rust-analyzer is installed but NOT enabled. It resolves Rust types and
    call targets far more precisely than the built-in tree-sitter analyzer.
      Enable per run:  --backend rust-analyzer
      Or by env:       HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1
      Indexing runs this project's build scripts (build.rs) and proc macros
      as you -- the same trust you extend by opening it in an editor. Enable
      it only for repositories you trust.

WHY THE CAVEAT IS THE POINT. `rust-analyzer scip <workspace>` EXECUTES that
workspace's build.rs and expands its proc macros, as the invoking user,
exit 0, silently. Canary-verified. That is inherent to Cargo rather than a
hypergumbo defect -- it is the same trust model as opening the project in an
editor -- but hypergumbo is routinely pointed at repositories the user has
not read, so the capability must not be advertised without the consequence.

A SAFE MODE WAS ATTEMPTED AND FAILED; THE FAILURE IS RECORDED SO IT IS NOT
RE-ATTEMPTED BLIND. `--config-path` with `cargo.buildScripts.enable=false`
and `procMacro.enable=false` was accepted, reported no config errors, and
ran the build script anyway -- under BOTH the nested and the
`rust-analyzer.`-prefixed key spellings. My first reading that it worked was
Cargo CACHING the previous run's build-script output; on three fresh
never-built crates the canary fires in every arm:

    bare `rust-analyzer scip`                 -> build.rs RAN
    cargo.buildScripts.enable=false           -> build.rs RAN
    rust-analyzer.cargo.buildScripts.enable=false -> build.rs RAN

So there is no known way to index a Cargo project without running its code,
and the opt-in gate is load-bearing for SAFETY -- not only for the ~10x
indexing cost its own docstring cites, which is the weaker reason. Options
if this is ever revisited: sandbox the invocation, or generate a
rust-project.json so cargo is bypassed (losing dependency resolution).

WIRED, not merely written. `check_rust_analyzer_disclosure` is reached
through `check_partial_install_warnings`, the entry point the CLI already
calls, and a test asserts that -- otherwise it would be a WI-ratuv family
member: correct, tested, and called by nobody. Verified live on a real Rust
crate: the advisory appears on `hypergumbo survey`.

TEST-ISOLATION TRAP FIXED IN PASSING. The wiring test first used a
hand-rolled linker-context stub carrying only the attributes the aggregator
read WHEN NO LINKERS WERE REGISTERED. It passed alone and failed under the
full suite twice, where every package loads: first the JNI linker read
`ctx.symbols`, then gRPC's requirement check globbed `ctx.repo_root`. The
neighbouring tests pass `repo_root=None` and survive only because they PATCH
`check_linker_requirements`; this one exercises the real path deliberately.
It now uses production's own LinkerContext with a real empty directory, and
says so, so the None idiom is not copied into another real-path test.

